From dc01d1173f857d8a877affb841bdf1840e9bd1b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chad Holland <261277573+holland202@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 04:46:08 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Pin both UNKNOWN_KEY checks with fixtures (follow-up to #4)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

No fixture had rejectCategory UNKNOWN_KEY. In the Python reference verifier,
both UNKNOWN_KEY rejects (verify.py:242, kid not in the key set; :246, kid
names a key of another suite than the header's alg) can be made no-ops with
all 44 fixtures still passing.

Add two fixtures with the existing generator:
- cgt-compact-unknown-key: cgt-compact-valid's token unchanged, verifier
  key set without broker-key-1. REJECT, UNKNOWN_KEY, reason contains "not in".
- cgt-compact-kid-suite-mismatch: compact CGT, header alg EdDSA with kid
  broker-pqc-1 (an ML-DSA-65 key), genuine ML-DSA-65 signature.
  REJECT, UNKNOWN_KEY, reason contains "suite".

Both reference verifiers already pass both (46 of 46), so neither changes.
With line 242 or 246 made a no-op, the Python verifier no longer passes the
suite. Note: in Python each mutant fails by an uncaught exception on the new
fixture (KeyError at :243, AttributeError in suite_verify), not by a wrong
verdict; the fixtures pin the category for implementations where a missing
or mismatched key does not happen to raise. The spec references are our
choice (§9.3, §9.2, §9.5, §8.2); adjust as you see fit. MANIFEST.sha256,
conformance.json and the README table and counts (46) are updated.

Found with a mutation harness written and run by Claude (Opus 5.5, Anthropic);
I reviewed this change and am responsible for it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
